I wouldn't accuse that player of cheating if he or she quit at that point.
I wouldn't quit myself, though; I'd either decide to give up the conduct,
or keep looking for food until I starved to death.

> If one wizard can't whine that he can't get his magic missile (after
> all, he may have given up that charm monster spell, or identify, or
> something equally immensely useful), then the samuarai can't whine
> because he can't--this time around--show how cool and zen he is. If
> you set the standard, you have to stick by it.

I don't really even like the idea of re-rolling for the special case of the
zen samurai.  I still think it's a different case though, since you're
deliberately re-rolling so as to make the game harder (but not impossible).
The main reason I'm okay with re-rolling in this case is that it's such an
interesting challenge, with literary precedent.

Basically, what I mainly consider cheating here is re-rolling your
character *in order to make the game easier* for yourself, regardless of
what other challenges you are posing for yourself.

I don't think this is in the same league with other forms of cheating, such
as save-scumming, which is probably the lowest form of cheating I can think
of at the moment (and I have tried it myself once, by the way :) ).

